<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-III-pde" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
 <title>Partial Differential Equations </title>

<introduction>

      <p>
        A partial differential equation (PDE) is an equation involving an unknown multivariable function and 
        its partial derivatives with respect to two or more independent variables. 
         </p>
         <p>
          Suppose <m>u = f(x, y)</m> is an unknown function of two independent variables
                <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
         A general PDE relates <m>(x, y)</m>, the function <m>u</m>, and its partial
                derivatives such as 
                <md>\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}=u_x</md>,
                <md>\frac{\partial u}{\partial y} =u_y</md>,
                <md>\frac{\partial^{2} u}{\partial x^{2}} = u_{xx}</md>,
                <md>\frac{\partial^{2} u}{\partial x\partial y} = u_{xy}</md>, and
                <md>\frac{\partial^{2} u}{\partial y^{2}} = u_{yy} </md>:
                <md>
                    F\!\left(x, y, u, \frac{\partial u}{\partial x}, \frac{\partial u}{\partial y}, \frac{\partial^{2} u}{\partial x^{2}}, \frac{\partial^{2} u}{\partial x\partial y}, \frac{\partial^{2} u}{\partial y^{2}}, \dots\right) = 0
                </md>
         </p>
      <p>
        <md>\dfrac{\partial u}{\partial t}+ c\dfrac{\partial u}{\partial x}= 0</md> 
        is first order linear partial differential equation which is useful in transport mechanism. For example 
        when quantity <m>u</m> moves along the <m>x</m>-axis at constant speed <m>c</m> without changing shape.
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>\dfrac{\partial u}{\partial t}= \alpha \dfrac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2}</md> is a  second order linear PDE which is useful on analyzing heat equation. 
        For example, temperature or diffusion <m>u(x, t)</m> spreading over time with thermal diffusivity <m>\alpha \gt 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>\dfrac{\partial^2 u}{\partial t^2}= c^{2} \dfrac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2}</md> is a 2nd-order linear PDE which is a wave equation. 
        Vibrations or wave propagation (e.g., a string or acoustic wave) with wave speed <m>c</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>\dfrac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2}+ \dfrac{\partial^2 u}{\partial y^2}= 0</md> is a 2nd-order linear PDE which is Laplace's Equation. 
        Steady-state temperature, electrostatic potential, or incompressible irrotational flow.
      </p>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Show that the function <m>u(x,y)=e^x \sin y</m>  is a solution of Laplace's equation.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            We first compute the second-order partial derivatives: 
              <m>u_x= e^x \sin y </m>,  <m>u_y= e^x \cos y </m>,   <m>u_{xx}= e^x \sin y </m>, <m>u_{yy}= - e^x \sin y </m>, 
              so,
              <md>
                u_{xx} + u_{yy} = e^x \sin y - e^x \sin y = 0
              </md>
              Therefore <m>u</m> satisfies Laplace's equation.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Verify that the function <m>u(x,t) =\sin (x-at) </m> satisfies the wave equation.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <md>
              u_x = \cos (x-at)   \quad \text{ and } \quad  u_t = -a \cos (x-at)
            </md>
             <md>
              u_{xx} = -\sin (x-at)   \quad \text{ and } \quad  u_{tt} = a^2 \sin (x-at) = a^2 u_{xx}
            </md>
            <md>
              \therefore \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial t^2} = a^2 \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2}
            </md>
            
          </p>
        </solution>
      </example>

</introduction>


<subsection xml:id="subsec-tangnt_plane">
  <title>Tangent Planes and Linear Approximations</title>
  <p>
    In single-variable calculus, a differentiable function <m>y = f(x)</m> has a
        tangent line at <m>x_{0}</m> that approximates the curve locally:
        <md>
            L(x) = f(x_{0}) + f'(x_{0})(x - x_{0})
        </md>
        In multivariable calculus, a differentiable function of two variables
        <m>z = f(x, y)</m> defines a surface <m>S</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}^{3}</m>.
        At a point <m>P_{0}(x_{0}, y_{0}, z_{0})</m> on <m>S</m>, the best flat
        approximation to the surface is the tangent plane.
  </p>
  <image source="tangent-plane.jpeg" width="30%"/>
    <p>
      <term>Equation of the Tangent Plane:</term>  Using the normal vector
            <m>\mathbf{n}= \langle a, b, c \rangle</m> and point
            <m>P_{0}(x_{0}, y_{0}, z_{0})</m>, the general scalar equation of a plane is:
            <md>
                a(x - x_{0}) + b(y - y_{0}) + c(z - z_{0}) = 0
            </md>
            Substituting <m>a = f_{x}(x_{0}, y_{0})</m>, <m>b = f_{y}(x_{0}, y_{0})</m>, and
            <m>c = -1</m>, and solving for <m>z</m>, yields the tangent plane equation for
            <m>z = f(x, y)</m> at <m>(x_{0}, y_{0})</m>:
            <md>
                z - z_{0} = f_{x}(x_{0}, y_{0})(x - x_{0}) + f_{y}(x_{0}, y_{0})(y - y_{0})
            </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      <term>Linear Approximation and Differentials:</term>
      Rewriting the right-hand side defines the linearization (tangent plane approximation) of 
      <m>f</m> at <m>(x_{0}, y_{0})</m>:
            <md>
                L(x, y) = f(x_{0}, y_{0}) + f_{x}(x_{0}, y_{0})(x - x_{0}) + f_{y}(x_{0}, y_{0})(y - y_{0})
            </md>
            For <m>(x, y)</m> close to <m>(x_{0}, y_{0})</m>, we write
            <m>f(x, y) \approx L(x, y)</m>.
            Setting <m>\Delta x = x - x_{0}</m> and <m>\Delta y = y - y_{0}</m>, the total
            differential is:
            <md>
                dz = f_{x}(x_{0}, y_{0})\,dx + f_{y}(x_{0}, y_{0})\,dy
            </md>
            which estimates the change
            <m>\Delta z = f(x_{0} + \Delta x, y_{0} + \Delta y) - f(x_{0}, y_{0})</m> along
            the tangent plane.
    </p>
    <p>
      <term>Level Surfaces and the Gradient:</term> When a surface is defined implicitly as a level
            surface <m>F(x, y, z) = k</m> rather than explicitly as <m>z = f(x, y)</m>, the
            gradient vector
            <m>\nabla F(x_{0}, y_{0}, z_{0}) = \langle F_{x}, F_{y}, F_{z} \rangle</m> is
            normal to the surface at <m>P_{0}(x_{0}, y_{0}, z_{0})</m> (provided
            <m>\nabla F \neq \mathbf{0}</m>).The tangent plane equation in implicit form is:
            <md>
                F_{x}(x_{0}, y_{0}, z_{0})(x - x_{0}) + F_{y}(x_{0}, y_{0}, z_{0})(y - y_{0}) + F_{z}(x_{0}, y_{0}, z_{0})(z - z_{0}) = 0
            </md>
            Setting <m>F(x, y, z) = f(x, y) - z = 0</m> recovers
            <m>\nabla F = \langle f_{x}, f_{y}, -1 \rangle</m>, matching the explicit
            formula above.
    </p>





    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the equation of the tangent plane to the paraboloid 
                    <m>z = f(x, y) = x^{2} + 2y^{2}</m> at the point <m>(1, -1, 3)</m>, and use it
                    to approximate <m>f(1.05, -0.96)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute partial derivatives:
                                <md>
                                    f_{x}(x, y) = 2x, \qquad f_{y}(x, y) = 4y
                                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Evaluate at <m>(x_{0}, y_{0}) = (1, -1)</m>:
                                <md>
                                    f_{x}(1, -1) = 2(1) = 2, \qquad f_{y}(1, -1) = 4(-1) = -4
                                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Write the tangent plane equation:
                                <md>
                                    z - 3 = 2(x - 1) - 4(y + 1)
                                </md>
                                Simplifying gives <m>z = 2x - 4y - 2</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Approximate <m>f(1.05, -0.96)</m>:
                                <md>
                                    L(1.05, -0.96) = 3 + 2(0.05) + (-4)(0.04) = 3 + 0.10 - 0.16 = 2.94
                                </md>
                                (Exact value: <m>(1.05)^{2} + 2(-0.96)^{2} = 1.1025 + 1.8432 = 2.9457</m>.)
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <definition xml:id="def-linear-approx">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>z=f(x,y) </m> then <m>f</m> is differentiable at <m>(a,b) </m> if <m>|Delta z</m> can be expressed in the form 
          <md>
            \Delta z= f_x(a,b)\Delta x +  f_y(a,b)\Delta y +\epsilon_1 \Delta x + \epsilon_2 \Delta y  
          </md>
          Where <m>\epsilon_1</m> and <m>\epsilon_2</m> <m>\to 0 </m> as <m>(\Delta x, \Delta y) \to (0,0).</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-diff-function">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If the partial derivatives <m>fx</m> and <m>fy</m> exist near <m>(a,b)</m> and are continuous at 
          <m>(a,b)</m>, then <m>f</m> is differentiable at <m>(a,b)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
    
   <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface
                <m>z = x^{2} + 2xy + y^{2}</m> at the point <m>(1, 1, 4)</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>L(x, y)</m> be the linear approximation of
                            <m>f(x, y) = \sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}</m> at <m>(3, 4)</m>.
                            What is <m>L(3.1, 3.8)</m>?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              A surface is defined implicitly by <m>x^{2} y + yz^{3} + xz = 5</m>.
                            What is a normal vector to the tangent plane at <m>(1, 1, 1)</m>?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>f(x, y)</m> has continuous first partial derivatives with 
              <m>f(2, -1) = 7</m>, <m>f_x(2, -1) = -3</m>, and <m>f_y(2, -1) = 5</m>, what is the differential <m>dz</m> at <m>(2, -1)</m>?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The radius <m>r</m> and height <m>h</m> of a right circular cylinder are
                            measured as <m>r = 10\text{ cm}</m> and <m>h = 20\text{ cm}</m>, with possible
                            errors of <m>\Delta r = 0.1\text{ cm}</m> and <m>\Delta h = -0.2\text{ cm}</m>.
                            What is the differential estimate <m>dV</m> of the change in volume
                            <m>V = \pi r^{2} h</m>?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              At what point <m>(x_{0}, y_{0})</m> is the tangent plane to
                            <m>z = x^{2} + 3y^{2} - 4x + 6y</m> horizontal (parallel to the
                            <m>xy</m>-plane)?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use the linear approximation of <m>f(x, y) = e^{x - 2y}</m> at <m>(2, 1)</m> to
                            estimate <m>f(2.03, 0.99)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If the tangent plane to <m>z = f(x, y)</m> at <m>(1, 2)</m> has equation
                            <m>z = 5 + 3(x - 1) - 4(y - 2)</m>, what is the directional derivative
                            <m>D_{\mathbf{u}}f(1, 2)</m> in the direction of
                            <m>\mathbf{u}= \langle \frac{3}{5}, \frac{4}{5}\rangle</m>?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Find the linearization <m>L(x, y)</m> of <m>f(x, y) = \ln(2x + y)</m> at <m>(1, 0)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
         </p>
    </statement>
   </example>


