</subsection>


<subsection xml:id="subsec-implicit-diff">
  <title>Implicit Differentiation</title>
  <p>
    Implicit differentiation finds the derivative <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m> of an
        equation relating <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> without solving for <m>y</m> first.
        You treat <m>y</m> as an implicit function of <m>x</m> and apply the chain rule
        whenever you differentiate a term containing <m>y</m> with respect to <m>x</m>.
  </p>
  <image source="impli-diff.jpeg" width="30%"/>
    <p>
      <em>Why Use Implicit Differentiation?</em>
      Many curves fail the vertical line test for <m>y</m>, such as circles, ellipses, or mixed expressions
            like <m>x^{3} + y^{3} = 6xy</m>.
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <alert>Explicit form:</alert> <m>y</m> is isolated on one side, such as
                                    <m>y = \sqrt{25 - x^{2}}</m> for the top half of a circle.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <alert>Implicit form:</alert> <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> appear together in an equation, such as
                                    <m>x^{2} + y^{2} = 25</m>, which describes the entire circle at once.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
            Notice how the circle diagram above illustrates this cleanly: at <m>x = 3</m>,
                        there are two points: <m>(3, 4)</m> and <m>(3, -4)</m> with opposite tangent
                        slopes (<m>-\frac{3}{4}</m> and <m>\frac{3}{4}</m>).
                        The derivative <m>\frac{dy}{dx}= -\frac{x}{y}</m> captures both using both
                        coordinates.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because <m>y</m> depends on <m>x</m>, differentiating <m>y^{n}</m> with respect
            to <m>x</m> requires the chain rule:
            <md>
                \frac{d}{dx}\bigl[y^{n}\bigr] = n y^{n-1}\frac{dy}{dx}
            </md>
            When differentiating mixed products like <m>xy</m>, apply the product rule along
            with <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m>:
            <md>
                \frac{d}{dx}\bigl[xy\bigr] = 1 \cdot y + x \cdot \frac{dy}{dx}= y + x\frac{dy}{dx}
            </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>The Implicit Function Formula</term>
      If <m>F(x, y) = 0</m> defines <m>y</m>
            implicitly as a differentiable function of <m>x</m>, and <m>F</m> has continuous
            first partial derivatives with <m>F_{y}(x, y) \neq 0</m>, then applying the multivariable 
            chain rule to <m>w = F(x, y)</m> where  <m>y = y(x)</m> gives:
            <md>
                \frac{dw}{dx}= \frac{\partial F}{\partial x}\frac{dx}{dx}+ \frac{\partial F}{\partial y}\frac{dy}{dx}= 0
            </md>
            Since <m>\frac{dx}{dx}= 1</m>, solving for <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m> yields the
            shortcut formula:
            <md>
                \frac{dy}{dx}= -\frac{F_{x}}{F_{y}}
            </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      <term>Geometric Meaning: </term> 
      The equation <m>F(x, y) = 0</m> represents a level curve of the
            two-variable surface <m>z = F(x, y)</m> at height <m>0</m>.The gradient vector
            <m>\nabla F(x, y) = \langle F_{x}, F_{y} \rangle</m> is always perpendicular
            (normal) to the level curve <m>F(x, y) = 0</m>.A tangent vector along the curve
            has direction <m>\langle dx, dy \rangle</m>.Because the gradient and tangent
            vectors are perpendicular, their dot product is zero:
            <md>
                F_{x}\,dx + F_{y}\,dy = 0 \implies \frac{dy}{dx}= -\frac{F_{x}}{F_{y}}
            </md>
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m> for <m>x^{3} + y^{3} = 6xy</m> using partial derivatives.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Set <m>F(x, y) = 0</m>:
                                <md>
                                    F(x, y) = x^{3} + y^{3} - 6xy = 0
                                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find <m>F_{x}</m> and <m>F_{y}</m>:
                                <md>
                                    F_{x} = 3x^{2} - 6y \qquad F_{y} = 3y^{2} - 6x
                                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Apply the formula:
                                <md>
                                    \frac{dy}{dx}= -\frac{3x^{2} - 6y}{3y^{2} - 6x}= \frac{2y - x^{2}}{y^{2} - 2x}
                                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      If <m>F(x, y, z) = 0</m>
      When an equation <m>F(x, y, z) = 0</m> defines <m>z</m> implicitly as a function
            of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, the same chain rule argument gives both partial
            derivatives of <m>z</m>:
            <md>
                \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}= -\frac{F_{x}}{F_{z}}\qquad \text{and}\qquad \frac{\partial z}{\partial y}= -\frac{F_{y}}{F_{z}}\quad (F_{z} \neq 0)
            </md>
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\frac{\partial z}{\partial x}</m> and
                    <m>\frac{\partial z}{\partial y}</m> if 
                    <m>x^{3} + y^{3} + z^{3} + 6xyz = 1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Define <m>F(x, y, z) = 0</m> Move all terms to the left side:
                    <md>
                        F(x, y, z) = x^{3} + y^{3} + z^{3} + 6xyz - 1 = 0
                    </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          For <m>\frac{\partial z}{\partial x}</m>:
                    <md>
                        \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}= -\frac{F_{x}}{F_{z}}= -\frac{3x^{2} + 6yz}{3z^{2} + 6xy}= -\frac{x^{2} + 2yz}{z^{2} + 2xy}
                    </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          For <m>\frac{\partial z}{\partial y}</m>:
                    <md>
                        \frac{\partial z}{\partial y}= -\frac{F_{y}}{F_{z}}= -\frac{3y^{2} + 6xz}{3z^{2} + 6xy}= -\frac{y^{2} + 2xz}{z^{2} + 2xy}
                    </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
</subsection>
</section>